  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 356 PM EDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES WV imagery places the upper level ridge axis over the \nPlains with two elongated closed lows on either side of the ridge \nover the Pacific Northwest and the Gulf of St Lawrence. At the sfc, \nRAP analysis has high pressure over eastern Canada, sending ridging \ninto the Great Lakes. Not much is expected to change with this \npattern through Sunday with an overall warm and pleasant Labor Day \nweekend expected. With the high pressure ridge building gradually \noverhead and sfc troughing over the Northern Plains, the pressure \ngradient remains weak with light northeast to east flow. As a \nresult, dry weather persists through Monday and Lake Breezes are \nexpected today and Sunday. Highs this afternoon through Monday peak \nin the 70s with interior locations observing low 80s Sunday and \nMonday. With little cloud cover and calm conditions tonight, \nradiative cooling brings temps down into the 40s away from the \nlakeshores with upper 40s to mid 50s at the shores. Fog development \nis expected again tonight; patchy dense fog down to 1/4 mile is \npossible in the central and eastern UP into Sunday morning (~30% \nchance). As the ridge shifts east and troughing deepens over the \nPlains, southerly flow increases cloud cover on Monday and continues \nthe warming trend for overnight lows. Temps settle into the mid 50s \nto mid 60s Monday night and upper 50s to 60s Tuesday night.\n\nPrecip chances return Monday night from the west, continuing into \nTuesday night as the upper level ridge moves east over the Great \nLakes and flattens. This gives way to the deep western trough to \nlift northeast to Manitoba by Tuesday afternoon, which then tracks \neast to Quebec through Wednesday. A resulting sfc low from the \nnorthern Rockies lifts to Manitoba for Tuesday, then moves across \nfar northern Ontario to Quebec through Wednesday. Showers return to \nthe UP ahead of the associated cold front, mainly on Tuesday. \nInstability struggling to reach 500 j/kg mean strong to severe \nstorms are not expected. Widespread rainfall amounts have trended \ndown as deterministic solutions are indicating QPF splits somewhat \naround the UP. Probabilities of at least 1 inch of rain in 24 hours \nare now 30-60% only over the north half and far western Upper \nPeninsula, quickly falling to 10-25% elsewhere. This system also \nyields breezy winds. Initially expecting south winds on Tuesday \nahead of the cold front gusting between 15-30 mph, particulary in \nideal southerly downslope flow. Behind the front, pressure rises and \nCAA support stronger westerly winds on Wednesday with frequent gusts \nof 20-30 mph. Probabilities for gusts exceeding 40 mph are between \n25-50% from Marquette to Whitefish point and Saxon Harbor to \nOntonagon. Could see a Wind Advisory being needed in the Keweenaw \nwhere probabilities for gusts exceeding 45 mph already are between \n50-70%. \n\nWeak high pressure ridging returning to the area bring back much \ncalmer conditions on Thursday. The drying trend in the forecast has \nalso removed lingering precip chances until the weekend, although \nthe marginally cold enough 850 mb temps could yield scattered lake \neffect cloud cover late Wednesday night into Thursday. Otherwise \ntemps return near normal in the upper 60s to mid 70s late next week.",
